    "57670": {
        "order_id": 18,
        "exam_type": "WAEC",
        "subject": "Chemistry",
        "topic": "Acid-Base Theories",
        "question_type": "Objective",
        "exam_year": "2019",
        "question_text": "<p>Which of the following is a <b>Bronsted-Lowry base</b> but not an Arrhenius base?</p>",
        "question_image": null,
        "answer_image": null,
        "explanation": "<p>A Bronsted-Lowry base is a proton ($H^+$) acceptor, while an Arrhenius base is a substance that dissociates in water to produce $OH^-$ ions. Ammonia ($NH_3$) is a Bronsted-Lowry base because it accepts protons but does not produce $OH^-$ directly, so it is not an Arrhenius base.</p>",
